{
  "work_package": "08 production-shaped round check (500 games/round)",
  "measured_at": "2026-09-30",
  "question": "Does active/batch=512 make sense when a round only contains 500 games?",
  "answer": {
    "batch_cap": "A round of 500 games caps the active pool and therefore the batch at 500 rows (one pending leaf per world); a 512-wide buffer can never be filled, so active/batch=512 is meaningless for this round shape. The earlier 512/512 window measurement used 700 games per worker and is a steady-state shape, not the production round.",
    "but_bigger_active_pays": "Raising active from 256 to 500 still cuts the round from 926 s to 802-822 s (+13-15% games/min) because the round then runs as one pool instead of two waves, and the mean batch rises from 210 to 220-391.",
    "batch500_vs_256": "500/500 (802 s) and 500/256 (822 s) differ by only 2.5%, inside single-run noise; 500/256 keeps the smaller buffer (GPU peak 49 vs 82 MiB) with essentially the same round time.",
    "window_vs_round": "Steady-state 35 s windows reported 54.2k sim/s at 256 and 58.5k at 512; the complete 500-game rounds average 35.7k/41.2k/40.2k sim/s because of the pool tail plus per-game validation and archiving. Window numbers overstate production round efficiency."
  },
